# @@title@@ for Windows

A WinUI 3 app on .NET 10. People download it from the product's website
(`https://@@name@@.<apps domain>/downloads`): an installer, or a ZIP to run without installing.
Both update themselves from the website, which [Velopack](https://velopack.io) makes possible.

- `App/`: the app: sign-in, account deletion and updates (`App/Updates.cs`).
@@if payments@@
- Plans and prices open the website's pricing page: people buy there, with its order button and
  their right of withdrawal, and the app sees the purchases on their account.
@@end@@
- `Core/`: everything that is not UI, including the client of the server's API. Kiota generates it
  into `Core/Api` from `../server/openapi.json` on every build (`dotnet tool restore` first), so it
  always matches the server's snapshot; it is not committed.
- `Tests/`: the tests of `Core`.

On Windows with the .NET 10 SDK:

```powershell
dotnet tool restore
dotnet test Tests/Tests.csproj
dotnet publish App/App.csproj -c Release -p:Platform=x64 -p:RuntimeIdentifier=win-x64 -o publish
dotnet tool run vpk pack --packId @@namespace@@ --packVersion 1.0.1 --packDir publish `
    --mainExe @@namespace@@.exe --packTitle "@@title@@" --icon App/App.ico --runtime win-x64 `
    --outputDir releases
```

`releases/` then holds `@@namespace@@-win-Setup.exe` (the installer),
`@@namespace@@-win-Portable.zip` (the ZIP), and the files the updates need. Forge Web does all of
this on its Windows machines, signs the files when a signing machine is set up, and puts them on
the website after an admin's approval. Every version must be higher than the one before.

`Core/Server.cs` says where the server runs: staging while the app is tested, production in the
release build, and where the website offers the downloads. Replace `App/App.ico` with your own
icon.
